---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import BlogLead from '@/components/blog/BlogLead.astro'
import BlogBody from '@/components/blog/BlogBody.astro'
import BlogRelated from '@/components/blog/BlogRelated.astro'

const post = {
 title: 'Minimum Order Quantity for Embroidery and Screen Printing: What\'s Realistic',
 description:
 'Embroidery and screen-printing minimums vary by product, artwork, and method. Learn what drives small-batch pricing.',
 category: 'Operational',
 readTime: '7 min read',
 date: 'May 9, 2026',
 isoDate: '2026-05-09',
 image: '/images/og-default.jpg',
 slug: 'minimum-order-quantity-embroidery-screen-printing',
}

const faqs = [
 { q: 'What\'s the minimum order for custom embroidered shirts?', a: 'Your quote lists the minimum for the garment, artwork, and placement. Send your required quantity for a written quote.' },
 { q: 'What\'s the minimum order for screen printing?', a: 'Your quote lists the minimum for the garment, artwork, colors, and placements. Send your required quantity for a written quote.' },
 { q: 'Can I get a small custom shirt order?', a: 'A small order may be available through embroidery, screen printing, HTV, DTF, or sublimation. Product compatibility, minimums, setup, and pricing require a written quote.' },
 { q: 'Why do embroidery shops have minimums?', a: 'File preparation, thread setup, hooping, proofing, and machine preparation can create fixed work. The quote confirms any setup and how quantity affects unit pricing.' },
 { q: 'How do I get lower per-piece pricing?', a: 'Ask for written quantity breaks on the same garment and decoration specification. A larger order may lower unit pricing, but the result depends on current partner terms.' },
 { q: 'Can I combine multiple orders to hit a minimum?', a: 'Garment, artwork, color, and placement decide it. Ask us whether the items can share a run.' },
 { q: 'What\'s the difference between minimum order and minimum run?', a: 'Minimum order can refer to the total accepted quantity. Minimum run can apply to each garment, color, artwork, or placement combination. Confirm both in the quote.' },
]

const schema = {
 '@context': 'https://schema.org',
 '@graph': [
 { '@type': 'Article', headline: post.title, description: post.description, image: `https://bighornthreads.com${post.image}`, datePublished: post.isoDate, dateModified: '2026-11-11', author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Bighorn Threads', url: 'https://bighornthreads.com/' }, publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Bighorn Threads', logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: 'https://bighornthreads.com/images/logos/bighorn-logo-color.png' } }, mainEntityOfPage: `https://bighornthreads.com/blog/${post.slug}/` },
 { '@type': 'BreadcrumbList', itemListElement: [{ '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://bighornthreads.com/' }, { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Blog', item: 'https://bighornthreads.com/blog/' }, { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: post.title, item: `https://bighornthreads.com/blog/${post.slug}/` }] },
 { '@type': 'FAQPage', mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a } })) },
 ],
}

const speakableSchema = {
 '@context': 'https://schema.org',
 '@type': 'WebPage',
 url: `https://bighornthreads.com/blog/${post.slug}/`,
 name: post.title,
 speakable: {
  '@type': 'SpeakableSpecification',
  cssSelector: ['h1','.speakable-answer'],
 },
}
---

<BaseLayout title={post.title} description={post.description} canonical={`https://bighornthreads.com/blog/${post.slug}/`} ogImage={post.image} type="article" publishedDate={post.isoDate}>
 <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
 <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(speakableSchema)} />
 <article class="bg-concrete">
 <header class="post-head">
 <div class="post-head-inner">
 <div>
 <nav class="mb-6 font-mono text-xs text-ash" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/" class="hover:text-gold-700">Home</a><span class="px-2">/</span><a href="/blog/" class="hover:text-gold-700">Blog</a></nav>
 <span class="mb-3 inline-block text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-600">{post.category}</span>
 <h1 class="mb-6 font-heading text-3xl font-bold leading-tight tracking-tight sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl">{post.title}</h1>
 <p class="mb-8 text-xl leading-relaxed text-ash">Why MOQs exist, what drives them, how to get small-batch work done when you genuinely need it, and how to optimize larger orders for better per-piece pricing.</p>
 <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-4 gap-y-2 border-t border-hairline pt-6 font-mono text-sm text-ash"><span class="text-gold-600">Bighorn Threads Team</span><span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span>{post.readTime}</span><span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><time datetime={post.isoDate}>{post.date}</time></div>
 </div>
 <BlogLead />
 </div>
 </header>
 <BlogBody>

 <h2 id="realistic-minimums" class="mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">What are realistic minimum order quantities for embroidery and screen printing?</h2>
 <aside class="mb-10 border border-hairline bg-panel p-7">
 <p class="mb-3 font-mono text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-gold-600">Quick Answer</p>
 <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal sm:text-xl speakable-answer">At Bighorn Threads, the minimum depends on the garment, artwork, placement and decoration method, and your written quote lists it. Embroidery usually suits smaller orders because there are no screens to set up. Screen printing has setup for each color, so it pays off on larger runs. For a few pieces, DTF or HTV can work.</p>
 </aside>

 <h2 id="why-moqs" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">Why Minimum Orders Exist</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Custom apparel decoration has fixed setup costs that happen once per run regardless of quantity:</p>
 <ul class="mb-6 list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong>Digitizing</strong> converts approved artwork into an embroidery production file (see <a href="/blog/logo-digitizing-101-embroidery/" class="text-gold-600 underline decoration-gold-600/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">logo digitizing</a>). Setup, reuse, and retention terms require confirmation.</li>
 <li><strong>Screen preparation</strong> can require separate work for each color and run.</li>
 <li><strong>Ink mixing</strong> (screen printing): pantone color matching takes time and ink that isn't recoverable.</li>
 <li><strong>Hooping / press setup</strong>: the machine has to be threaded, tensioned, and registered for each run.</li>
 <li><strong>Color matching + proofing</strong>: making sure the output matches expectations.</li>
 </ul>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Fixed setup work spreads across the approved quantity. The effect on unit pricing varies by method, artwork, and partner. Compare written quantity breaks when <a href="/screen-printing-vs-embroidery-construction-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-600 underline decoration-gold-600/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">choosing between screen printing and embroidery</a>.</p>

 <h2 id="alternatives" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">Alternatives for Small Batches</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">For runs under the embroidery/screen-print MOQ, four alternatives have different economics:</p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold">Heat-Transfer Vinyl (HTV)</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Good for a few shirts with simple logos, names, or numbers. It is cut and pressed one piece at a time, so it gets slow on big runs.</p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold">Direct-to-Film (DTF)</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Good for small runs of full-color logos, since there are no screens to burn. See <a href="/blog/dtf-printing-vs-screen-printing-custom-apparel/" class="text-gold-600 underline decoration-gold-600/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">DTF vs. screen printing</a> for how the two compare.</p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold">Sublimation (polyester only)</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Sublimation dyes the fabric itself, so it works on light-colored polyester and leaves no feel on the shirt. Our <a href="/blog/sublimation-printing-custom-apparel-guide/" class="text-gold-600 underline decoration-gold-600/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">sublimation printing guide</a> covers when it fits.</p>

 <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 font-heading text-xl font-bold">Small-batch embroidery with surcharge</h3>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Small embroidery runs may be possible, sometimes with a small-order charge. Ask us for the number on your item.</p>

 <h2 id="optimizing-larger" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">Optimizing Larger Orders for Better Per-Piece Pricing</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Three ways to get a better price without ending up with boxes of extras:</p>
 <ul class="mb-6 list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong>Batch planned reorders.</strong> Ask for the next price break, then decide if you would really use the extra shirts.</li>
 <li><strong>Standardize specifications.</strong> Reusing the same garment, artwork, and placement may reduce setup work. The quote confirms whether the partner can combine the run.</li>
 <li><strong>Consider a <a href="/company-stores/" class="text-gold-600 underline decoration-gold-600/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">company store</a>.</strong> Employees order approved gear in their own sizes, and you avoid guessing at a box of extras.</li>
 </ul>

 <h2 id="other-factors" class="mt-12 mb-4 font-heading text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">Other Cost Factors Beyond Quantity</h2>
 <p class="mb-6 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Even within a single quantity tier, three factors move the per-piece price meaningfully:</p>
 <ul class="mb-6 list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">
 <li><strong>Stitch count or color count.</strong> A simple bold logo embroiders cheaper than a detailed multi-color one. A single-color screen print costs much less than a 6-color print. See <a href="/blog/how-much-to-embroider-a-shirt/" class="text-gold-600 underline decoration-gold-600/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">how much to embroider a shirt</a> for the full pricing breakdown.</li>
 <li><strong>Garment cost.</strong> Basic and protective garments can have very different prices. Compare current quotes for the exact products.</li>
 <li><strong>Decoration placements.</strong> A single left-chest logo costs less than a chest + sleeve + back combination because each placement is a separate press operation.</li>
 </ul>

 <div class="mt-12 border border-hairline bg-gold-500/5 p-7 sm:p-9">
 <h2 class="mb-3 font-heading text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-charcoal">Need to figure out the right order size?</h2>
 <p class="mb-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-charcoal">Bighorn Threads offers embroidery, screen printing, and other decoration methods. The quote confirms the current minimum, setup, unit pricing, and any quantity break for the selected specification. See <a href="/services/screen-printing-las-vegas/" class="text-gold-700 underline decoration-gold-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-gold-600">custom screen printing in Las Vegas</a> for how print orders are quoted.</p>
 <a href="/get-a-quote/" class="inline-flex min-h-[48px] items-center gap-2 bg-gold-500 px-7 py-3 text-base font-bold text-charcoal transition-all hover:bg-gold-600">Get a Quote →</a>
 </div>
 </BlogBody>
 </article>
<BlogRelated />
</BaseLayout>
